 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the distribution of data and the sparsity of the isosurface. In the worst case, it may visit many nodes, but pruning based on min/max bounds and LOD keys significantly reduces the search space compared to a full traversal.
--   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of nodes in the 3D binary tree, as the tree structure must be stored.
--   **Strengths:** Efficiently isolates cells at a specific resolution, reducing the number of triangles generated and processed. Leverages multikey indexing for fast pruning.
--   **Limitations:** Requires the data to be pre-processed into a 3D binary tree with min/max/LOD keys. Performance degrades if the isosurface is extremely complex or if the LOD distribution is uneven.
--   **When to use:** When rendering isosurfaces at a specific level of detail is required, and the data is stored in a multidimensional tree structure.
--   **When not to use:** When the entire volume needs to be rendered at all LODs simultaneously without culling, or if the tree construction cost outweighs the search savings for small datasets.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the distribution of data and the density of the isosurface. In the worst case, it may traverse significant portions of the tree, but pruning based on min/max bounds and LOD keys reduces this significantly [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of nodes in the LOD Tree. The tree structure itself requires storing min, max, and LOD keys for each cell [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficiently isolates cells at a specific resolution, avoiding the cost of processing higher or lower LODs. Optimizations for key boundaries reduce unnecessary checks [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Performance degrades if the isosurface intersects many cells across all LODs or if the tree is unbalanced. The algorithm assumes a pre-constructed 3D binary search tree [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When extracting isosurfaces from large voxel datasets where only a specific level of detail is required, and the data is structured in a multikey search tree.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When the isosurface is sparse and scattered across many LODs, or when the tree construction cost outweighs the search savings for a single query.
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the DYNAISOLOD algorithm, which combines dynamic isosurface extraction with level-of-detail techniques in voxel space. It was developed to improve the performance of isosurface rendering by avoiding the generation of triangles for cells that do not intersect the isosurface or are at an inappropriate resolution. The approach builds on the Marching Cubes algorithm, applying it on a cell-by-cell basis only to the relevant cells found via the tree search. [S1, unofficial]
-
+The method originates from the DYNAISOLOD system, which generates triangles on a cell-by-cell basis using the Marching Cubes algorithm for each cell found [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to handle dynamic isosurface extraction and level-of-detail in voxel space, optimizing for parallel file creation and compact storage via bit-mapped vertex coordinates, specifically targeting structured (rectilinear) grids [S1, unofficial].
